// The icon Windows Explorer shows for the executable itself. Separate from the Qt
// resource beside it: that one is read once the application is running, while a file
// browser only ever looks at the binary's own resource section.
//
// A numeric id, not the IDI_ICON1 seen in most examples. That symbol comes from
// windows.h, which is not included here, so it would be recorded as a *named* resource
// — and Explorer shows the icon with the lowest numeric id, ignoring named ones. The
// result is a binary that carries a perfectly good icon and still shows the default.
1 ICON "kobayashi.ico"
